  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "gen_move",
    "file": "mruby/mrbgems/mruby-compiler/core/codegen.c",
    "description": "The peephole rewrite in `gen_move` incorrectly groups `OP_LOADI32` with three-byte-operand instructions and re-emits it using `genop_3(s, data.insn, dst, data.b, data.c)`. `OP_LOADI32` has BSS encoding (one 8-bit register plus two 16-bit immediate halves), while `genop_3` emits BBB and takes an 8-bit `c`. This truncates the immediate and shortens the instruction from 6 bytes to 4, corrupting alignment of all following bytecode. The VM subsequently decodes operand bytes as opcodes; in this PoV it reaches a spurious `OP_LOADL` whose pool index is read beyond the instruction stream and then accesses `pool[0]` despite `plen == 0`, causing the heap-buffer-overflow at `mrb_vm_exec` in `src/vm.c:1139`.",
    "conditions": "The fuzzer copies attacker bytes into a NUL-terminated Ruby source string and calls `mrb_load_string`. A source expression must generate `OP_LOADI32` into a temporary register and then move/assign that result so `gen_move`'s peephole optimization retargets the load (`nopeep` false, last instruction is `OP_LOADI32`, its destination equals the move source, and that source is not a local register). This occurs for `\\xff=91327;9`: 91327 is outside the 16-bit immediate range but inside int32, and assignment triggers the rewrite. Subsequent emitted instructions are misaligned; their bytes decode as a bogus `OP_LOADL` against the empty literal pool, producing the out-of-bounds read."
  },
